 Creating a complex smart contract involves writing a set of rules and conditions that govern the execution of transactions on a blockchain network. Smart contracts are self-executing contracts with the terms of the agreement between buyer and seller being directly written into lines of code.

Here's an example of a complex smart contract, using Solidity language for Ethereum blockchain:

```solidity
pragma solidity ^0.8.0;

contract RealEstateAuction {
    address public owner;
    mapping (address => uint256) public bids;
    uint256 public auctionEnd;
    uint256 public propertyId;

    constructor(uint256 _propertyId, uint256 _auctionEnd) public {
        owner = msg.sender;
        propertyId = _propertyId;
        auctionEnd = _auctionEnd;
    }

    function bid(uint256 _bid) public payable {
        require(msg.sender != owner, "Cannot bid on your own property.");
        require(_bid > bids[msg.sender], "Your bid must be greater than the previous bid.");
        bids[msg.sender] = _bid;
    }

    function endAuction() public {
        require(block.timestamp > auctionEnd, "Auction has not ended yet.");
        address highestBidder = address(0);
        uint256 highestBid = 0;

        for (address bidder in bids) {
            if (bids[bidder] > highestBid) {
                highestBid = bids[bidder];
                highestBidder = bidder;
            }
        }

        require(highestBidder != address(0), "No bids were made.");
        owner.transfer(highestBid);
    }
}
```

This smart contract represents an auction for a real estate property. It allows multiple participants to bid on the property, and the contract automatically assigns ownership to the highest bidder once the auction ends. The contract also includes conditions to prevent the owner from bidding on their own property and to ensure that each bid is higher than the previous one. Additionally, the contract includes a function to transfer the winning bid amount to the contract owner.

