 In the Bitburner game, you can indeed access the return value of a script. To do so, you can use a function called `os.run()` and capture its output using the `os.execSync()` function. The `os.run()` function runs a script and returns a Promise that resolves to an object containing the script's exit code and the output of the script. On the other hand, `os.execSync()` runs a script and returns the output of the script as a string.

Here's an example of using `os.run()` to execute a script and access its return value:

```javascript
const { exec } = require("os");

function runScript(scriptName) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    exec(scriptName, (error, stdout, stderr) => {
      if (error) {
        reject(error);
      } else {
        resolve(JSON.parse(stdout));
      }
    });
  });
}

runScript("myScript.js").then(result => {
  console.log("Return value:", result);
}).catch(error => {
  console.error("Error:", error);
});
```

For more details about Bitburner scripting, you can refer to the official documentation: https://bitburner.io/docs/scripting/api/os

