 In JavaScript, you can reverse a string using the built-in `split()`, `reverse()`, and `join()` methods. Here's a simple function that demonstrates this:

```javascript
function reverseString(str) {
  return str.split('').reverse().join('');
}

// Usage
console.log(reverseString("Hello, World!")); // Output: "!dlroW ,olleH"
```

This function works by splitting the input string into an array of characters, reversing the order of the array elements using the `reverse()` method, and then joining the reversed elements back into a single string using the `join()` method. Keep in mind that this function does not handle non-string inputs, so you should validate the input before passing it to the function.

Alternatively, you can use the `reduce()` method to achieve the same result:

```javascript
function reverseString(str) {
  return str.split('').reduce((rev, char) => char + rev, '');
}

// Usage
console.log(reverseString("Hello, World!")); // Output: "!dlroW ,olleH"
```

The `reduce()` method applies a function against an accumulator and each element in the array (which is your string split into characters), reducing them to a single output value. In this case, the function concatenates each character to the accumulator in reverse order, resulting in the reversed string.

